---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Nav from '../components/Nav.astro';
---

<Layout
  title="How it works — MeshWhisper"
  description="The two-layer architecture, namespace isolation, shared relay mesh, and privacy properties of MeshWhisper."
>
  <Nav current="How it works" />

  <!-- Page header -->
  <div class="border-b border-slate-800 bg-slate-900/40">
    <div class="max-w-6xl mx-auto px-6 py-16 text-center">
      <h1 class="text-4xl font-bold text-white mb-4">How it works</h1>
      <p class="text-lg text-slate-400 max-w-xl mx-auto">
        The core insight is simple. The consequences are significant.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <main class="max-w-6xl mx-auto px-6 py-20">
    <div class="max-w-2xl mx-auto space-y-20">

      <!-- Core insight -->
      <section>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-4">The core insight</h2>
        <p class="text-slate-400 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          A relay does not need to read a message to forward it. If messages are encrypted before they leave
          the sending device, a relay is just a pipe. It receives an opaque blob, identifies the intended
          recipient by a hash, and forwards it. The message arrives at the recipient's device and is decrypted
          there, locally, with a key the relay never held.
        </p>
        <p class="text-slate-400 leading-relaxed mb-6">
          The consequence is that the relay infrastructure for one application is identical — in every
          technical respect — to the relay infrastructure for every other application. A relay forwarding
          encrypted packets for a fitness app is indistinguishable from one forwarding for a marketplace.
          The relay cannot tell them apart. They use the same relay.
        </p>
        <div class="bg-slate-900 border border-slate-700 rounded-xl px-6 py-4 font-mono text-brand-300 text-sm text-center">
          Relay promiscuously. Connect selectively.
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- Namespace isolation -->
      <section>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-4">Namespace isolation</h2>
        <p class="text-slate-400 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          Applications sharing relay infrastructure are isolated cryptographically, not by access control.
          Each application is identified by a namespace derived from the developer's public key and the
          application bundle identifier. Destination hashes — the routing identifiers on every packet — are
          computed as:
        </p>
        <div class="bg-slate-900 border border-slate-800 rounded-xl p-5 font-mono text-sm text-brand-300 my-6 text-center">
          BLAKE3(namespace_id || peer_key || epoch_hour)
        </div>
        <p class="text-slate-400 leading-relaxed">
          A relay node cannot determine which application a packet belongs to, cannot inject into any namespace
          without knowing the identifier, and cannot correlate traffic across namespaces even under active
          observation. The isolation is structural. Hashes rotate every hour, limiting traffic-analysis windows.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- Node layer -->
      <section id="node-layer">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-4">The node layer</h2>
        <p class="text-slate-400 leading-relaxed mb-6">
          The node layer is the backbone. When a developer integrates the SDK, they deploy a MeshWhisper Node —
          a single lightweight binary providing four functions in one container:
        </p>
        <div class="space-y-3 mb-6">
          {[
            ['Packet relay and store-and-forward', 'Routes encrypted packets to connected clients. Queues blobs for offline recipients with a configurable TTL (default 72 hours).'],
            ['Push notification forwarding', 'When a message arrives for an offline device, fires a silent content-free wake signal via APNs, FCM, or Web Push. The signal contains no message content.'],
            ['Encrypted media storage', 'Accepts and serves opaque encrypted blobs for media messages. Never holds a decryption key. TTL-based expiry.'],
            ['Prekey directory', 'Stores public pre-key bundles for X3DH first contact. Allows strangers to initiate a session without prior communication.'],
          ].map(([title, body]) => (
            <div class="bg-slate-900 border border-slate-800 rounded-lg p-4">
              <div class="text-white font-medium text-sm mb-1">{title}</div>
              <div class="text-slate-400 text-sm leading-relaxed">{body}</div>
            </div>
          ))}
        </div>
        <p class="text-slate-400 leading-relaxed">
          The node handles what phones cannot: always on, always connectable, holds messages for offline
          recipients. It is a post box, not a postman.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- Device layer -->
      <section id="mesh">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-4">The device layer</h2>
        <p class="text-slate-400 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          Every device running any SDK-embedded application is simultaneously a potential relay for every
          other such device. The mesh emerges from participation — it is not deployed, it accumulates.
        </p>
        <p class="text-slate-400 leading-relaxed mb-6">
          When two devices are nearby they relay directly using platform-native P2P (Apple Multipeer
          Connectivity on iOS, Google Nearby Connections on Android). When further apart but on the same
          network, they relay over the local subnet. Over the internet, connectable devices — laptops on
          broadband, desktops — act as peers. Phones on cellular maintain outbound connections to their
          deployed node.
        </p>
        <div class="bg-slate-900 border border-yellow-800/40 rounded-xl p-5 text-sm text-slate-400">
          <span class="text-yellow-500 font-medium">Roadmap.</span> The device mesh layer is specified and
          partially scaffolded in the SDK. The node layer is fully implemented and live.
          The mesh emerges in Stage 3 as SDK adoption grows.
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- Privacy -->
      <section>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-4">Privacy that strengthens with adoption</h2>
        <p class="text-slate-400 leading-relaxed mb-6">
          This is the inverse of centralised messaging, where scale concentrates metadata.
          In MeshWhisper, scale distributes it.
        </p>
        <div class="space-y-4 mb-6">
          {[
            ['Low density', 'Messages route Alice → Alice\'s node → Bob\'s node → Bob. Each node sees one end of the path. Privacy is roughly equivalent to a messaging service provider\'s view.'],
            ['High density', 'Messages route through multiple intermediate nodes. No single relay can correlate sender and recipient. Privacy approaches a backbone ISP\'s view: aware that traffic flows, unable to say meaningfully between whom.'],
            ['Always', 'Every active device generates a continuous stream of random encrypted packets — chaff — at a constant rate regardless of real message activity. A node operator cannot distinguish real messages from noise.'],
          ].map(([label, body]) => (
            <div class="flex gap-4">
              <div class="text-slate-600 font-mono text-xs w-24 shrink-0 pt-0.5">{label}</div>
              <p class="text-slate-400 text-sm leading-relaxed">{body}</p>
            </div>
          ))}
        </div>
        <p class="text-slate-500 text-sm leading-relaxed">
          MeshWhisper is not anonymous communication in the strong sense — it is not Tor.
          The protocol's privacy properties are real, bounded, and transparent about what they don't provide.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- Bootstrap -->
      <section>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-4">The bootstrap problem, honestly</h2>
        <p class="text-slate-400 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          The node layer has value from the first integration — a developer who deploys a node gets working
          E2EE relay infrastructure immediately, before the mesh exists. That is a real outcome.
        </p>
        <p class="text-slate-400 leading-relaxed">
          The distinctive value is in the mesh layer, and the mesh requires adoption that does not exist on
          day one. This is the BitTorrent dynamic honestly applied. What the architecture ensures is that
          each stage of adoption delivers real, usable value. The ceiling scales with adoption;
          the floor is set by the node layer on day one.
        </p>
      </section>

      <div class="border-t border-slate-800 pt-8 flex justify-between text-sm">
        <a href="/" class="text-slate-500 hover:text-slate-300 transition-colors">← Home</a>
        <a href="/security" class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300 transition-colors">Security →</a>
      </div>

    </div>
  </main>

</Layout>
